  "transcript": "# Gibraltar's New Deal with the EU: Sovereignty, Borders, and Beyond\n\n## Why Gibraltar Needed a Separate Deal\n> Gibraltar was left out of the 2020 trade deal, so a bespoke treaty was needed to keep the border open and avoid economic disruption.\n\nChair: Minister, why wasn't Gibraltar included in the 2020 trade and co-operation agreement? And why did it take five years to get this new agreement?\nLord Wood of Anfield: Gibraltar wasn't in the TCA because the EU, probably pushed by Spain, wanted separate arrangements. The bridging measures that followed kept the border fluid informally, but they weren't sustainable. Businesses couldn't plan, and the EU's new entry/exit system meant Spain couldn't keep them going. So we needed a treaty.\nLord Wood of Anfield: Why five years? First, it's complex—over 1,000 pages, many dossiers, physical infrastructure. Second, politics: we had turmoil in the UK in 2022, a Spanish election in 2023, and a change of government here in 2024. That slowed things down.\nCommittee Member: You talk about 'fluidity at the frontier'. How is that different from free movement?\nLord Wood of Anfield: Fluidity means the 15,000 people who cross the land border daily can do so without checks. That's the key to Gibraltar's economy. It's not free movement—there are different arrangements for air and sea arrivals.\n\n\n## Sovereignty: Red Lines and Reassurances\n> The treaty explicitly protects UK sovereignty over Gibraltar, and any alignment with EU rules is a choice by Gibraltar, not a concession.\n\nCommittee Member: The treaty involves dynamic alignment with EU rules. Does that threaten UK sovereignty, especially with Spain getting powers over arrest, residency, and borders?\nLord Wood of Anfield: No, sovereignty isn't diminished. Article 2 explicitly says the treaty doesn't affect the sovereignty position. These are technical arrangements about Gibraltar's relationship with the EU. Gibraltar chose to align in certain areas to get the benefits of an open border.\nCommittee Member: But you said there are 43 annexes of EU law applying to Gibraltar. That sounds like a lot.\nLord Wood of Anfield: Actually, most of those annexes aren't about EU law—they're procedural or lists. Only about eight contain EU law references, and Gibraltar opted into those. It's a bespoke deal that avoids imposing irrelevant EU law.\nCommittee Member: What about Spain's role? Could it creep into more control over Gibraltar?\nLord Wood of Anfield: No, the scope is carefully delineated. For example, Spanish military officers only deal with military derogations and limited information sharing. There's no jurisdiction for them to expand. In fact, more cooperation is what makes the treaty work.\n\n\n## Military Base and Schengen Checks\n> The treaty protects UK military operational autonomy, with special arrangements for personnel and goods, and Schengen checks won't trap military staff in the 90-day rule.\n\nCommittee Member: Will this treaty affect military personnel and access to the naval base?\nLord Wood of Anfield: Safeguarding operational autonomy was a red line. There's no Spanish role in authorising UK military operations or deployments. Military personnel, civilians on defence business, and their families aren't subject to routine Schengen checks.\nLord Wood of Anfield: We do have limited information sharing with Spanish military liaison officers to facilitate military derogations. The Commander British Forces is positive about how it's working.\nCommittee Member: What about the Schengen 90-day rule? Could military staff get caught up in that when visiting other Schengen countries?\nLord Wood of Anfield: There's now an arrangement so military personnel can clock in and out, stopping and starting the Schengen clock appropriately. That's an implementation improvement in the last couple of months.\n\n\n## Entry Decisions and Mutual Recognition\n> Gibraltar keeps control over who enters, but it has agreed to mutually recognise Spanish refusals into Schengen based on security concerns, which raised questions about what happens if the two disagree.\n\nCommittee Member: Who decides who can be refused entry into Gibraltar? Could Spain effectively block someone?\nLord Wood of Anfield: Gibraltar has been in charge of entry for decades, and that continues. Gibraltar residents can't be refused entry. Everyone arriving at the airport goes through Gibraltar immigration, then Spanish Schengen checks.\nLord Wood of Anfield: If someone is flagged in the Schengen Information System, Spain may refuse them entry into Schengen. Gibraltar has decided to mutually recognise those refusals based on security or public safety. But Gibraltar still makes its own decision about entry into Gibraltar.\nCommittee Member: So if Gibraltar says no but Spain says yes, what happens? Could someone be allowed into Spain but not Gibraltar?\nLord Wood of Anfield: Conceptually, yes. Gibraltar controls entry to Gibraltar. But we'll get back to you on the specific case of non-residents. The Chief Minister can also give you a practical answer.\nChair: That's an important point—please do come back to us with clarity.\n\n\n## Costs, Concordat, and Termination\n> The UK won't pay for the financial mechanism—Gibraltar will—and the concordat is a political, not legal, document; termination clauses include a Spanish option only after a four-year review.\n\nCommittee Member: How much will Gibraltar pay for this, and will the UK contribute?\nLord Wood of Anfield: Both parties will set up independent funds. Gibraltar will fund its own, and under the concordat, the UK contribution will come from Gibraltar, not UK taxpayers. No specific timelines yet, but it'll be set up soon.\nCommittee Member: What's the legal status of the concordat? Is it as binding as the treaty?\nLord Wood of Anfield: It's a political agreement, not legally binding. It sets out how the UK and Gibraltar will work together on implementation. It respects constitutional arrangements and was wanted by Gibraltar.\nPaul Hughes: It's a political instrument between us and the Government of Gibraltar.\nChair: So it can be changed by political whim. That's a contrast to the treaty's enforcement mechanisms.\nLord Wood of Anfield: Any UK Government will want a good relationship with Gibraltar, and Gibraltar will want clarity. So I expect it to endure.\nCommittee Member: On termination: there seem to be three routes—Article 334 with 12 months' notice and Gibraltar consent, Article 19.5 with 90 days if Gibraltar rejects an EU law update, and Spain unilaterally forcing termination. Is that right?\nLord Wood of Anfield: Article 19.5 is precedented in EU agreements with Norway, Switzerland, and Iceland. It's for a very specific scenario where Gibraltar rejects an update, triggering a 90-day discussion period. It's designed to be resolved.\nPaul Hughes: The Spanish option is in the mobility chapter, after a four-year review. If Spain tells the EU it's no longer content, the EU can start termination. It's not at any time—it's tied to that review.\nChair: Is it a one-off option at that moment?\nPaul Hughes: I'm fairly certain, yes. We can confirm with the Chief Minister.\n\n\n## The Road Ahead\n> The treaty is the final piece of the Brexit puzzle, providing a stable foundation, but implementation will require ongoing cooperation and adjustments.\n\nCommittee Member: Does this agreement settle Gibraltar's future for good, or will more agreements be needed?\nLord Wood of Anfield: From a treaty perspective, it's the last piece of the jigsaw after Brexit. Sovereignty is fully protected. There will be implementation adjustments, but this is a stable and lasting foundation for Gibraltar's relationship with the EU.\nChair: Thank you both for your evidence. We're on time. It's been a pleasure.\n"
